[Music]  welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Alice Kaplan who is John M mustard  professor of French a specialist of 20th  century France  professor Kaplan works at the  intersection of literature and history  using a method that allies archival  research with textual analysis a writer  and a literary translator she served on  the advisory board of the National Book  Foundation's study of the state of  translation in the United States which  resulted in the establishment of a  National Book Award and translated  literature today we'll talk with  professor Kaplan about her recent book  titled looking for the stranger Albert  Camus and the life of a literary classic  as well as some of the issues involved  in translating books  welcome professor Kaplan glad to be here  let's start with an overview of your  book tell us about it  looking for the stranger is just that  I'm trying to account for the life of  this mysterious and wonderful book  Albert Camus the stranger I think  probably since the 1950s this has been  the French novel that our students read  not just to learn French because it's  often taught for example to teach the  past tense but to learn how to be a  thinking person okay it's it has a  unique status in the Canon of French  literature and there have been hundreds  thousands maybe interpretations of the  novel and I thought what would happen if  instead of giving one more  interpretation I told the history of the  book how it was made under what  conditions was it published how did it  become such a phenomenal success across  the globe it was published at the very  worst moment for French publishing in  the depths of the Nazi occupation and  camera was an unemployed and very ill  ill with tuberculosis writer in the  boondocks of Algeria it really is a  miracle book yes it sounds fascinating  and and why do you think it's such an  iconic book to the French  well if first of all there's it's style  it is it it's almost changed the  evolution of the novel it's written in a  very flat and plain language and the  narrator more so expresses very little  emotion until the end of the book and  you try to understand who is this guy  what is he really thinking you see  nothing of his interior you only see his  response to the world and there's  something about it that makes the world  around him so clear and that makes  people long to understand him so he is  known in France and and in the United  States too as the ultimate  existentialist character I say and what  led you to write this book well I think  I'm not very different than most  Americans who study French the very  first novel I ever read in French  proudly was Kim as the stranger I read  it in a French camp in Bar Harbor Maine  when I was 15 years old it's stuck with  me I think I've read it a hundred times  since I've taught it at all levels and  it's it's the book of my life and how  did you do the research and and doing  the research did anything strike you  differently after having read the books  so many times and that you learn  something new what I've always said is  that I was taught the stranger with  absolutely no knowledge of the fact that  it took place in Algeria and you know  during the 90s you couldn't go to  Algeria because they were in the midst  of a very violent civil war  now you can so the first thing I did  when I started working on the book was  to go to Algiers where Cameron grew up  and to look at all the places where he  had lived both when he was living in the  setting of the novel  in this working-class neighborhood of  his childhood and the places where he  lived when he wrote the novel and seeing  those places changed everything for me  and what way I could imagine him  absorbing the the ambience of Algiers of  working-class Algiers and I could go on  the beach where the famous fight took  place between Rosso and the Arab that  resulted in the death of the Arab it was  some of it is almost impossible to  describe just a feeling of being close  to the writer and then of course I also  read all his correspondence during the  period when he was writing and his  Diaries he kept not not intimate Diaries  but work-in-progress Diaries so you  could actually see the way the book  almost came alive without him even  intending it he was working on another  book altogether and he's suddenly seen  these images of a man condemned to death  and where are these Diaries now well  they're all published they're called  notebook skimmers notebooks there are  three volumes of them and they've been  translated all three volumes they're  just a wonderful tool for getting closer  to cameras process as a writer right  right so as someone and I know you are  fluent in French but as someone who is  not fluent in French reading and English  translation and you having read both  what are some of the issues that you  come across or some things that you  think of and I know in reading your book  you make note about the stranger the  title itself meaning you know an  outsider or a foreigner and maybe not so  much the stranger so those are great  questions because in fact there are two  titles for the stranger the American  edition is always called a stranger the  British is always called the outsider  now some people like to argue that more  so the main character is not a stranger  he's an outsider a kind of alienated  outsider in society and he's also a  French citizen  in a colonial society so these terms  become so complicated  the very first translator of the  stranger into English for both the  English and the American editions was a  man named Stuart Gilbert quite eminent  translator who translated mudhole and  other great writers he really tried to  make the book sound normal more normal  than it is so kami wrote a lot of his  sentences in indirect discourse and  Stuart Gilbert liked to change those  into quoted material so you actually  hear the voice of a person saying  something and Kimi was really shocked  when he first saw the American edition  he paged through it and he said I don't  remember so many quotation marks in the  original and it was unfaithful that  translation was unfaithful then in the  1980s a very gifted translator named  Matthew Ward retranslated the novel and  that's the Edition that I would  recommend to people he understands all  the odd things chemin was doing with  language interesting I was going to ask  you what you think the best version of  this so I'm glad you told us that as a  translator what are some of the things  that you consider when you're working on  it on translating something mm-hmm well  you know the the saying goes that only  3% of the books published in the United  States are translations and I think that  is probably a stronger figure than the  real figure it is so hard to get a book  translated and so a lot of my energy  goes into talking to publishers about  books that I think are really wonderful  it's called there's an expression in  French m-pesa I'm a passer I try to to  get books to cross over into English I  was just working on a beautiful Algerian  novel called algae Lukie and it's a it's  really an essay about living in the city  of Algiers he talks about this  city of elders as a woman he says  Algiers she which works well in French  because the word for city is feminine  but we don't have the masculine and the  feminine so the first draft I had it all  as a she you know and my editors said I  don't know people might think you're  just not a very good speaker of English  and you don't understand we don't say  she we say it for the city so I had to  come up with a device to make the reader  understand that I knew that Algiers is  not as she in English but that it was a  intentional choice right so here at Yale  you've recently founded a new project  called the translation initiative top  tell us a little bit about that and what  you see as its mission and some of its  goals so the mission of the macmillan  translation initiative is to bring  issues of translation together in one  spot we did a study of the faculty i'm  working on this initiative with harold  OGG and Brahm we discovered that there  are something like three hundred and  sixty members of the faculty at all  levels at all schools all across the  campus whose research involves  translation in one way or another and  that translation is much bigger than  literary translation people in computer  science are very interested I mean as we  know there's now Google Translate so  they're fascinating theoretical issues  about whether you can build a  translation machine people in the  military are very concerned with  interpreting and translation I mean  there's not a field in which translation  doesn't live and so we are reaching out  to people across the campus we're  designing working groups were  publicizing events so that if somebody  is interested in translation in any way  they can come to us okay well we will  look forward to hearing more of that  initiative and I thank you for being  here with us  Dane's sharing some of your work it was  a pleasure and thank you to the  macmillan Maryland okay for more  information about professor kaplan and  her research please visit our website at  macmillan report yale.edu be sure to  join us again for another episode of the  Macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Center for International and  area studies at Yale  you

